  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "present simple: affirmative with be and have",
      "title_form": "Presente simple: afirmativo con be y have",
      "title_target": "Present simple: affirmative with be and have",
      "description_md_form": "El **presente simple** se usa para hablar de hechos, hábitos y estados permanentes. En esta lección lo usamos para describir el medio ambiente y expresar ideas generales.\n\nCon **be** (ser/estar), la forma cambia según el sujeto:\n\n- **I am** (I'm) → *I am worried about pollution.*\n- **He/She/It is** (He's) → *It is a big problem.*\n- **You/We/They are** (You're) → *They are important.*\n\nCon **have** (tener), usamos **have** para I/you/we/they y **has** para he/she/it:\n\n- *We have a lot of rubbish.*\n- *The city has a pollution problem.*\n\n**Recuerda**: en afirmativo, el verbo va directamente después del sujeto. No necesitas auxiliares como *do* o *does* en oraciones afirmativas.",
      "description_md_target": "The **present simple** is used to talk about facts, habits, and permanent states. In this lesson, we use it to describe the environment and express general ideas.\n\nWith **be**, the form changes according to the subject:\n\n- **I am** (I'm) → *I am worried about pollution.*\n- **He/She/It is** (He's) → *It is a big problem.*\n- **You/We/They are** (You're) → *They are important.*\n\nWith **have**, we use **have** for I/you/we/they and **has** for he/she/it:\n\n- *We have a lot of rubbish.*\n- *The city has a pollution problem.*\n\n**Remember**: in affirmative sentences, the verb goes directly after the subject. You don't need auxiliaries like *do* or *does* in affirmative sentences.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + be (am/is/are) + complement | Subject + have/has + noun",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "present_simple_be_have",
        "headers": [
          "Pronombre",
          "be",
